  <div id="lessonContent">
    <h2>1. What is a String?</h2>
    <p><b>String = Text.</b> Anything inside quotes is a string. <code>"Ali"</code>, <code>"Hello World"</code>, <code>"123"</code> - All are strings, even numbers inside quotes are strings.</p>
    <p><b>String Method = A Tool to Work With Text.</b> Like a toolbox - one tool cuts, one tool cleans, one tool changes case. JavaScript gives you many tools for text.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Important:</b> String methods <b>do NOT change the original string</b>. They return a new string. You must store the result in a variable. Example: <code>name.toUpperCase()</code> alone does nothing. You must do <code>let big = name.toUpperCase();</code></div>
    <h2>2. LIVE DEMO - Name Cleaner</h2>
    <p>Type your name with spaces and small letters. See all methods at once:</p>
    <div class="demo-card">
      <h3 id="demoClean" style="margin:0">Your Clean Name Will Show Here ✨</h3>
      <p id="demoDetails" style="color:#64748b;margin:8px 0;line-height:1.8">Length, Uppercase, Lowercase will show here</p>
      <input class="demo-input" id="nameInput" type="text" placeholder="Type name - e.g.   ali ahmed  " value="  ali ahmed  ">
      <button class="btn btn-js" onclick="cleanName()">Clean My Name 🧹</button>
      <button class="btn btn-next" onclick="makeUpper()">UPPERCASE 🔠</button>
      <button class="btn" onclick="makeLower()">lowercase 🔡</button>
      <button class="btn" onclick="resetDemo()">Reset ↺</button>
    </div>
    <h2>3. All String Methods - Full Table</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Method</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.length</b></td><td>Count letters (Not a method, a property - No brackets)</td><td>"Ali".length</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.toUpperCase()</b></td><td>Make ALL CAPITAL</td><td>"ali".toUpperCase()</td><td>"ALI"</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.toLowerCase()</b></td><td>Make ALL small</td><td>"ALI".toLowerCase()</td><td>"ali"</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.trim()</b></td><td>Remove spaces from start and end</td><td>"  Ali  ".trim()</td><td>"Ali"</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.slice(start, end)</b></td><td>Cut a part</td><td>"Hello".slice(0, 3)</td><td>"Hel"</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.replace(old, new)</b></td><td>Replace one word with another</td><td>"Hello Ali".replace("Ali", "Sara")</td><td>"Hello Sara"</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.split(" ")</b></td><td>Break text into array</td><td>"Ali Ahmed".split(" ")</td><td>["Ali", "Ahmed"]</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.includes("Ali")</b></td><td>Check if text contains this?</td><td>"Hello Ali".includes("Ali")</td><td>true</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.charAt(0)</b></td><td>Get letter at position</td><td>"Ali".charAt(0)</td><td>"A"</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>.indexOf("i")</b></td><td>Find position of letter</td><td>"Ali".indexOf("i")</td><td>2</td></tr>
    </table>
    <h2>4. length, toUpperCase, toLowerCase, trim</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">let name = "Ali";</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(name.length); // 3 - A, l, i</span><br><br>
<span class="text">let small = "ali ahmed";</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(small.toUpperCase()); // "ALI AHMED"</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(small.toLowerCase()); // "ali ahmed"</span><br><br>
<span class="text">let messy = "   Ali   ";</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(messy.trim()); // "Ali" - Spaces removed</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(messy.length); // 9 - Original still has spaces</span>
    </code></div>
    <p><b>Real Use:</b> When a user types their name in a form, they often add extra spaces or small letters. You use <code>trim()</code> + <code>toUpperCase()</code> to clean it before saving.</p>
    <h2>5. slice - Cut Text</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">let msg = "Hello World";</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(msg.slice(0, 5)); // "Hello" - From 0 to 5 (5 not included)</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(msg.slice(6)); // "World" - From 6 to end</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(msg.slice(-5)); // "World" - Last 5 letters</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Real Use - Show first 3 letters of password, hide rest</span><br>
<span class="text">let password = "MySecret123";</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(password.slice(0, 3) + "******"); // "MyS******"</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>6. replace, split, includes</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">let text = "I love HTML";</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(text.replace("HTML", "JavaScript")); // "I love JavaScript"</span><br><br>
<span class="text">let fullName = "Ali Ahmed Khan";</span><br>
<span class="text">let parts = fullName.split(" "); // ["Ali", "Ahmed", "Khan"]</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(parts[0]); // "Ali" - First name</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(parts[2]); // "Khan" - Last name</span><br><br>
<span class="text">let email = "ali@gmail.com";</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(email.includes("@")); // true - Valid email must have @</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(email.includes("yahoo")); // false</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>7. Complete Example - Email Checker + Username Maker</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;body&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;h1</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"result"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Check Your Name</span><span class="tag">&lt;/h1&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;input</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"fullName"</span> <span class="attr">type</span>=<span class="val">"text"</span> <span class="attr">placeholder</span>=<span class="val">"Your Full Name"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"makeUsername()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Make Username</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">function makeUsername() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let name = document.getElementById("fullName").value.trim().toLowerCase();</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let username = name.replace(" ", "_"); // "ali_ahmed"</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let first = name.split(" ")[0]; // "ali"</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = "Username: " + username + " | First Name: " + first + " | Length: " + name.length;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">}</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/body&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/html&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>8. Common Mistakes</h2>
    <p>1. <b>Forgetting Brackets:</b> <code>toUpperCase</code> without () does nothing. Correct is <code>toUpperCase()</code>.<br>2. <b>length is NOT a method:</b> <code>name.length</code> ✅, <code>name.length()</code> ❌ - No brackets for length.<br>3. <b>Not Storing Result:</b> <code>name.toUpperCase(); console.log(name);</code> - Still shows small letters. You must do <code>let big = name.toUpperCase();</code></p>
    <h2>9. Homework</h2>
    <p>In Playground, build this:</p>
    <p>1. Input - Full Name. Show: Trimmed name, Uppercase name, Length, First name using split(" ")[0]<br>2. Input - Email. Check with includes("@") - If true show "Valid Email", else show "Invalid - Must have @"<br>3. Input - Sentence. Replace one word with another word using replace()</p>
    <div class="box"><b>Remember:</b> String = Text in quotes | length = Count, no brackets | toUpperCase() = ALL CAPITAL | toLowerCase() = all small | trim() = Remove start/end spaces | slice(start, end) = Cut part | replace(old, new) = Change word | split(" ") = Break into array | includes() = Check if contains, true/false | Methods do NOT change original, store result in variable</div>
    <p><b>Next Step:</b> <a href="/lesson30">Lesson 30 - DOM</a> - Learn how JavaScript talks to HTML. Change any element, any style, from JS.</p>
